helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3156023	14			0	kid	qf3z+b‖This section provides the fundamentals for working with $[officename] Basic.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3147560	2			0	kid	0-05-2‖$[officename] Basic code is based on subroutines and functions that are specified between \<emph\>sub...end sub\</emph\> and \<emph\>function...end function\</emph\> sections. Each Sub or Function can call other Subs and Functions. If you take care to write generic code for a Sub or Function, you can probably re-use it in other programs. See also \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020300.xhp\" name=\"Procedures and Functions\"\>Procedures and Functions\</link\>.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id314756320				0	kid	a=iz+d‖Some restrictions apply for the names of your public variables, subs, and functions. You must not use the same name as one of the modules of the same library.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150398	3			0	kid	wrutnx‖What is a Sub?				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797	4			0	kid	2a0stu‖\<emph\>Sub\</emph\> is the short form of \<emph\>subroutine\</emph\>, that is used to handle a certain task within a program. Subs are used to split a task into individual procedures. Splitting a program into procedures and sub-procedures enhances readability and reduces the error-proneness. A sub possibly takes some arguments as parameters but does not return any values back to the calling sub or function, for example:				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3150868	15			0	kid	j[7]-9‖DoSomethingWithTheValues(MyFirstValue,MySecondValue)				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156282	5			0	kid	0s[252‖What is a Function?				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3156424	6			0	kid	2r=[ae‖A \<emph\>function\</emph\> is essentially a sub, which returns a value. You may use a function at the right side of a variable declaration, or at other places where you normally use values, for example:				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985	7			0	kid	+2v902‖MySecondValue = myFunction(MyFirstValue)				20130829 23:02:48
