helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119	41			0	kid	bmfzg5‖To export to ASP, in a $[officename] Impress document choose \<emph\>File - Export\</emph\>. You then see the \<emph\>Export\</emph\> dialog in which you select \<emph\>HTML Document\</emph\> as the file type. Once you have selected a directory and entered a file name, click \<emph\>Export\</emph\>. For export as ASP, we recommend selecting a "secret" file name for the HTML file (see below for more details). You then see the \<emph\>HTML Export\</emph\> dialog. Several files will be written to the directory you have just selected.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149410	42			0	kid	+u40sq‖The presenter uses the entered file name to change between the slides viewed by the audience. You can save the WebCast files locally or save them directly to an HTTP server. You can later transfer locally saved files to the HTTP server by FTP. Note that WebCast only works if the files are requested over an HTTP server.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3157974	43			0	kid	qrl3n6‖Do not use the same directory for two different HTML exports.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153157	44			0	kid	4]4s7m‖Select \<emph\>WebCast\</emph\> as a publishing type on the second page of the HTML Export Wizard.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154790	45			0	kid	d7l0ah‖In the options area for WebCast, select the \<emph\>Active Server Pages (ASP)\</emph\> option. You can now continue defining other settings or start the export by clicking the \<emph\>Create\</emph\> button.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153281	46			0	kid	225wvr‖Using ASP WebCast				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149910	47			0	kid	d=dox+‖You can use WebCast as soon as the exported files can be accessed from an HTTP server.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154503	48			0	kid	0hpsvi‖\<emph\>Example\</emph\>:				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152375	49			0	kid	n]vh+b‖Let's assume that you installed the Microsoft Internet Information Server on your computer. You entered the "c:\\Inet\\wwwroot\\presentation" directory as an HTML output directory during the IIS setup. The URL of your computer is assumed as follows: "http://myserver.com".				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715	50			0	kid	vxeefn‖You have saved the files that have been created during the Export process in the c:\\Inet\\wwwroot\\presentation\\ directory. In this directory, the Export creates an HTML file that can be named, for example, as "secret.htm". You entered this name in the Save dialog (see above). The presenter can now browse to the HTML Export files by entering the http://myserver.com/presentation/secret.htm URL in any HTTP Browser having JavaScript support. The presenter is now able to modify the page using some form controls.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159110	51			0	kid	wkps+l‖The audience can now view the slide selected by the presenter through the URL http://myserver.com/presentation/webcast.asp. They cannot move to other slides found at this URL, unless the file names are known. Please ensure that the HTTP server does not show the directory listing.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153732	52			0	kid	i9qx5n‖WebCast over Perl				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152999	53			0	kid	pmgv1y‖Exporting				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146972	54			0	kid	6auhwb‖To export, in a $[officename] Impress document choose \<emph\>File - Export\</emph\>. This opens the \<emph\>Export\</emph\> dialog, in which you select \<emph\>HTML Document\</emph\> as the file type. After selecting a folder and entering a file name, click \<emph\>Save\</emph\>. This opens the \<emph\>HTML Export Wizard\</emph\>. This will write some files to the folder you have just selected.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156362	55			0	kid	z4]ugq‖The entered file name will be used by the presenter to switch through the slides. Please select an empty directory.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148432	56			0	kid	ku7rua‖In the second page of the HTML Export, select \<emph\>WebCast\</emph\> as the publication type.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149018	57			0	kid	6d+13i‖In the option area for WebCast, select \<emph\>Perl\</emph\>.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154509	58			0	kid	41jxrw‖In the \<emph\>URL for listeners\</emph\> text box, enter the file name of the HTML document that will be used by the audience. In \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\>, enter the URL of the directory that will be used for the presentation and, in \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\>, enter the URL for the CGI script directory. You can now define further settings on the following pages of the Wizard or start the export process by clicking the \<emph\>Create\</emph\> button.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148996	59			0	kid	7z7v73‖Using Perl WebCast				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159268	60			0	kid	c9sbpm‖The files that have been created during the export must now be set up in the Perl enabled HTTP server. This cannot be done automatically because of the variety of different HTTP servers having Perl support. The steps to follow will be described next. Please refer to your server manual or ask your network administrator how to apply these steps on your server.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147340	61			0	kid	squag+‖You should first move the files that have been created during the export into the correct directory on the HTTP server.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155936	62			0	kid	j89p[-‖Move all files having the htm, jpg and gif extensions into the directory on your HTTP server that has been referred to in the text box \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\>.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151014	63			0	kid	4b-vm9‖All files having the pl and txt extensions have to be moved into the directory on your HTTP server that has been referred to in the \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\> text box. This directory has to be configured in a way that the Perl scripts contained there can also be run by an HTTP request.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151250	64			0	kid	7s6u0]‖On UNIX systems grant the files with the pl extension the rights to be executable by the HTTP server. Normally, this is done with the chmod command. The rights of the currpic.txt file must be set to be writable by the HTTP server.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153534	65			0	kid	xsb0d6‖Now you should be able to use WebCast.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156711	66			0	kid	5ev14b‖\<emph\>Example\</emph\>:				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147313	67			0	kid	37kkx6‖In this example, you have a Linux computer with an HTTP server. The URL of your HTTP server is http://myserver.com and the output directory of your HTML documents is the //user/local/http/ directory. Your Perl scripts are contained in the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory. Enter secret.htm as an export file name and presentation.htm as \<emph\>URL for listeners\</emph\>. In the \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\> text box enter http://myserver.com/presentation/ and for the \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\> enter http://myserver.com/cgi-bin/.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145792	68			0	kid	b46an4‖Now, copy all *.htm, *.jpg and *.gif files from the directories that were specified during the export into the //user/local/http/presentation/ directory on your HTTP Server and copy all files with the *.pl and *.txt extensions into the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153920	69			0	kid	ka][t9‖Login on your server as root and switch to the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory. You can define the corresponding rights using the chmod command.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148479	70			0	kid	rsi[59‖Once you have finished installing the Perl files, the presenter will be able to give the presentation. The listeners can view this presentation under the URL http://myserver.com/presentation/presentation.htm.				20130829 23:02:48
