helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10632				0	kid	8xomt=‖In %PRODUCTNAME, you can digitally sign your documents and macros. 				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id6564531				0	kid	b]d]6b‖Certificates				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10639				0	kid	y6ou8c‖To sign a document digitally, you need a personal key, the certificate. A personal key is stored on your computer as a combination of a private key, which must be kept secret, and a public key, which you add to your documents when you sign them.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066D				0	kid	q]al=z‖Save and sign the document				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10671				0	kid	vy5gt2‖When you apply a digital signature to a document, a kind of checksum is computed from the document's content plus your personal key. The checksum and your public key are stored together with the document.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10674				0	kid	y7jw=c‖Open a signed document				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10678				0	kid	o6q70]‖When someone later opens the document on any computer with a recent version of %PRODUCTNAME, the program will compute the checksum again and compare it with the stored checksum. If both are the same, the program will signal that you see the original, unchanged document. In addition, the program can show you the public key information from the certificate.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1067B				0	kid	nk+3mv‖You can compare the public key with the public key that is published on the web site of the certificate authority.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1067E				0	kid	98fbsv‖Whenever someone changes something in the document, this change breaks the digital signature. After the change, there will be no sign that you see the original document.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id2008200911381426				0	kid	685ny6‖The result of the signature validation is displayed in the status bar and within the Digital Signature dialog. Several documents and macro signatures can exist inside an ODF document. If there is a problem with one signature, then the validation result of that one signature is assumed for all signatures. That is, if there are ten valid signatures and one invalid signature, then the status bar and the status field in the dialog will flag the signature as invalid.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200911571878				0	kid	qcmp3w‖You can see any of the following icons and messages when you open a signed document.				20130829 23:02:48
