helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149054	4			0	kid	mt+mpi‖Braces "{}" are used to group expressions together to form one new expression. For example, "sqrt {x * y}" is the square root of the entire product x*y, while "sqrt x * y" is the square root of x multiplied by y. Braces do not require an extra space.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151392	5			0	kid	k=k5-[‖Set brackets were previously inserted in the Elements window or directly in the Commands window as "left lbrace <?> right rbrace". Now, a left and a right set bracket can also be inserted using "lbrace" and "rbrace", with or without wildcards.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147403	6			0	kid	mqbyw7‖There are a total of eight (8) different types of brackets available. The "ceil" and "floor" brackets are often used for rounding up or down the argument to the next integer: "lceil -3.7 rceil = -3" or "lfloor -3.7 rfloor = -4".				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146320	63			0	kid	nmdk]5‖Operator brackets, also known as Bra-kets (angle brackets with a vertical line in between), are common in Physics notation: "langle a mline b rangle" or "langle a mline b mline c over d mline e rangle." The height and positioning of the vertical lines always corresponds exactly to the enclosing brackets.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157870	7			0	kid	3i+jdb‖All brackets may only be used in pairs. The brackets have some common characteristics:				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155761	8			0	kid	duj[em‖All types of brackets have the same grouping function as described for "{}" brackets.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145590	9			0	kid	2+nsex‖All types of brackets, including those that are visible, permit empty group definition. The enclosed expression may therefore be empty.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154562	10			0	kid	j3zp-t‖Brackets do not adjust their size to the enclosed expression. For example, if you want "( a over b )" with a bracket size adjusted to a and b you must insert "left" and "right". Entering "left(a over b right)" produces appropriate sizing. If, however, the brackets themselves are part of the expression whose size is changed, they are included the size change: "size 3(a over b)" and "size 12(a over b)". The sizing of the bracket-to-expression ratio does not change in any way.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153002	11			0	kid	ttxqxe‖Since "left" and "right" ensure unique assignment of the brackets, every single bracket can be used as an argument for these two commands, even placing right brackets on the left side, or left brackets on the right. Instead of a bracket you can use the "none" qualifier, which means that there is no bracket shown and that there is no space reserved for a bracket. Using this, you can create the following expressions:				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150014	12			0	kid	jkpk2z‖left lbrace x right none				20130829 23:02:48
