helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145241	14			0	kid	f0f3a8‖left ] x right [				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156060	15			0	kid	pczzs7‖left rangle x right lfloor				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150935	16			0	kid	s4vi9o‖The same rules apply to "left" and "right" as to the other brackets: they also work as group builders and may enclose empty expressions.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149030	17			0	kid	t]9jn8‖The combination of mismatched brackets, single brackets and repositioned left and right brackets occurs often in mathematical formulas. The following is a formula that will create an error when typed:				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155989	19			0	kid	zqicak‖[2, 3) - right open interval				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147169	23			0	kid	o+ssp+‖Using "left" and "right" makes the above expression valid in $[officename] Math: "left [2, 3 right )". However, the brackets do not have any fixed size because they adjust to the argument. Setting a single bracket is a bit cumbersome. Therefore, there you can display single brackets with a fixed size by placing a "\\" (backslash) in front of normal brackets. These brackets then act like any other symbol and no longer have the special functionality of brackets; that is they do not work as group builders and their orientation corresponds to that of other symbols. See "size *2 \\langle x \\rangle" and "size *2 langle x rangle".				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153720	24			0	kid	1ga7b5‖The complete overview is as follows				20130829 23:02:48
