helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147514	33			0	kid	yj[8=k‖In this way, intervals like the one above can be built in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> without any problems: \\[2", "3\\) or "\\]2", "3\\[ (Attention: These quotation marks are part of the entry.)				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153532	34			0	kid	3mlj57‖Please note that the quotation marks must be entered and can be obtained with \<emph\>Shift+2\</emph\> and not with typographical quotation marks. Generally, punctuation marks (like the comma in this case) are set as text. Although it is also possible to type "\\[2,~3\\)" the above option is preferable. In the previous example, "fixed size" always describes a bracket size dependent on the font size used.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153674	35			0	kid	qdrpia‖Nesting groups within each other is relatively problem-free. In the formula hat "{a + b}" the "hat" is displayed simply over the center of "{a + b}". Also, "color red lceil a rceil" and "grave hat langle x * y rangle" work as expected. The result of the latter can be compared to "grave {hat langle x * y rangle}." These attributes do not compete, but rather can be combined.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147526	36			0	kid	lmk2gi‖This differs slightly for competing or mutually influencing attributes. This is often the case with font attributes. For example, which color does the b have in "color yellow color red (a + color green b)", or which size does it have in "size *4 (a + size /2 b)"? Given a base size of 12, does it have the size 48, 6 or even 24 (which could be seen as a combination)? The following are basic resolution rules, which will be followed consistently in the future. In general, the rules apply to all group operations. This only has a visible effect on the font attributes, like "bold", "ital", "phantom", "size", "color" and "font":				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152952	37			0	kid	8e+22c‖Group operations in sequence are treated as if every single operation is enclosed by braces. They are nested, and in every level there can be no more than one operation. Here is an example of a formula with many group operations: "size 12 color red font sans size -5 (a + size 8 b)" like "{size 12{color red{font sans{size -5 (a + {size 8 b})}}}}".				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158441	38			0	kid	448dp+‖This example formula is then interpreted from left to right. The operations only affect its corresponding group (or expression). Operations further to the right "replace" or "combine themselves with" their predecessors.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150994	39			0	kid	1w5v2o‖A group operation does not have any effect on higher-level operations but rather affects only lower-level groups and expressions, including their brackets and super-/subscripts. For example, "a + size *2 (b * size -8 c_1)^2"				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154196	40			0	kid	dmqp32‖"color ..." and "font ..." as well as "size n" (n is a decimal) replace any preceding operations of the same type				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154136	41			0	kid	z[bl81‖for "size +n", "size -n", "size *n", and "size /n" the effects of the operations are combined,				20130829 23:02:48
