helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150746	1			0	kid	dyh9x4‖\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091200.xhp\" name=\"Indexes and Exponents\"\>Indexes and Exponents\</link\>				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153730	2			0	kid	r9z0d[‖Here, you will find basic information about indexes and exponents in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\>. You can try the examples described here to help you understand the details discussed. (The quotation marks in this text are for emphasis purposes only and are not part of the examples.)				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149884	3			0	kid	b[d7+b‖The index and exponent for a character are displayed one on top of the other, left-justified to the base character. For example, type \<emph\>a_2^3\</emph\> or \<emph\>a^3_2\</emph\>. This can be in any order. Instead of \<emph\>'_'\</emph\> and \<emph\>'^'\</emph\>, you can use \<emph\>'sub'\</emph\> and \<emph\>'sup'\</emph\>.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148387	4			0	kid	w703t[‖However, it is no longer possible to use the following patterns				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149029	5			0	kid	8sd63c‖a_2_3				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155985	6			0	kid	kvcuce‖a^2^3				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153923	7			0	kid	ew2v02‖a_2^3_4				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153724	8			0	kid	kv2mzr‖Each sub-/superscript position of a base character can only be used once. You must use brackets to indicate the desired result. The following examples illustrate this				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151185	9			0	kid	7jgrgq‖a_{2_3}				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151272	10			0	kid	h8nwqj‖a^{2^3}				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156316	11			0	kid	5u9h0+‖a_2^{3_4}				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145207	12			0	kid	b+fpij‖a_{2^3}^{4_5}				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151173	13			0	kid	75k77k‖Unlike other formula editors where "\<emph\>_\</emph\>" and " \<emph\>^\</emph\> " only refer to the next character ("a_24" refers only to the "2"), $[officename] Math refers to the entire number(s)/name(s)/text. If you want to put superscripts and subscripts in sequence, the expression can be written as follows: a_2{}^3 or a^3{}_2				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154260	15			0	kid	uc5f9v‖To write tensors, \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> provides several options. In addition to the notation "R_i{}^{jk}{}_l", common in other applications, additional notations can be used, namely "R_i{}^jk{}_l" and "{{R_i}^jk}_l."				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147516	16			0	kid	42hkb]‖Super- and subscripts to the left of the base character can also be right-justified. To do this, the new commands "lsub" and "lsup" are used. Both commands have the same effect as "sub" and "sup", except that they are left of the base character. See also "a lsub 2 lsup 3."				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154276	17			0	kid	nmm8t9‖The rules governing unambiguity and the necessity of using brackets remain the same. In principle, this can be achieved with \<emph\>{}_2^3 a\</emph\>.				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152961	18			0	kid	8wu1+1‖The commands "sub" and "sup" are also available as "rsub" and "rsup".				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3158437	20			0	kid	oqfrv4‖Using the "csub" and "csup" commands, you can write super- and subscripts directly above or below a character. An example is "a csub y csup x". Combinations of indexes and exponents together are also possible: "abc_1^2 lsub 3 lsup 4 csub 55555 csup 66666."				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154570	21			0	kid	f3zbw9‖Super- and subscripts can be attached to most unary and binary operators. Two examples: "a div_2 b a<csub n b +_2 h" and "a toward csub f b x toward csup f y."				20130829 23:02:48
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155904	19			0	kid	fhccwb‖Be sure to also enter all spaces between characters when entering these examples into the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20130829 23:02:48
